dvi2tty and disdvi on OS-9 -- what was changed=============================================Marcel J.E. Mol's dvi2tty, from `dvi2tty.ar' in the Microware OS-9 archive.ORIG/ holds the release exactly as it came out of that archive, unpackedwith this disk's own `ar'.ALMOST NOTHING WAS CHANGED.  The one compile flag is `-dstrchr=index',because this C library calls that function `index'; the release's ownREADME predicted exactly that ("function strchr() can not be found: You areprobably a BSD UNIX or alike.  Solution: #define strchr index").The archive ALREADY CARRIED AN OS-9 MAKEFILE -- `Makefile' beside`makefile.org', with `cc -it=/r0', `-l=/dd/lib/os9lib.l' and a `chd RELS'.Somebody ported this to OS-9 in 1989 and it has been sitting in the archivesince.  What is here is a rebuild from that source with the collection'sdriver, so it is trap-free like everything else.WHAT THEY ARE FOR.  `dvi2tty' turns a .DVI -- TeX's typeset output -- intotext you can read on a terminal.  `disdvi' dumps the same file's structure:the preamble, the fonts it asks for, and every command, one to a line.  Thedisk has eight DVI drivers for printers and had nothing for a screen.ONE UNDOCUMENTED OPTION, from the author: `-e<n>' changes the spacingbetween words, positive to widen and negative to narrow.  He suggests`-e-11'; the OS-9 archive's own notes suggest `-e-15' for the files thatcame with the GNU compiler.THE README IS SHIPPED AS IT WAS WRITTEN, signature block and all.  It endswith four lines of Lennon (`Working Class Hero') in the author's .signature,which contains a word some would not choose.  It is his release and this ishow he sent it.